yupiter
-----------

yupiter is a library for the C programming language.

yupiter aims to be fast, customizable and safe, while maintaining
performance and control close to the traditional GNU C library
(glibc).

It is freestanding: it supplies its own entry point, its own formatted
output and its own allocator, and links without a C runtime.  Its
interface is ISO C17, usable from C and from C++; the implementation
behind that interface is ISO C++23, built freestanding.  The targets
are Linux on aarch64 and Linux on x86_64; any other operating system
or architecture is rejected when the project is configured, rather
than silently mapped onto an implementation that does not match it.

Project Status
-----------------

yupiter is stable.  The current release is 1.0.1 (see NEWS); the
shared object has SOVERSION 1.

Within the 1.x series the exported symbols, their signatures, their C
linkage and the public structure layouts do not change.  A change that
breaks any of them requires a new major version.  New functions, new
headers and new optional build features may be added in a minor
release without breaking existing consumers.

Build
-----------------

The build is a GNU Makefile driven by a POSIX configure script; no
CMake, Python or generator is required on the build host.

    ./configure
    make
    make check
    make install

"make install" refreshes the dynamic linker cache with ldconfig when
the shared object is installed into the running system.  A staged
installation with DESTDIR leaves the cache to the package manager.

"make" builds both libyupiter.a and libyupiter.so by default.  The
configure script detects Linux and the target architecture, checks
that the compiler can build the freestanding C++23 implementation, and
writes config.mk.  Run ./configure --help for the installation prefix,
the build type, the heap size and the options that select the static
library, the shared library and the tests.

The static archive contains the startup file and is meant for a
freestanding program linked with "-nostdlib".  The shared object
contains no entry point and is meant for a hosted program that already
has one.  Neither needs a C library or a C++ runtime.

Repository Structure
-----------------------
assembly/     Freestanding entry point and system-call trampolines
io/           Streams, formatting and the Linux system-call layer (C++23)
mem/          The allocator (C++23)
etc/          Templates of the package metadata
include/      Public headers and internal headers
docs/         Documentation
test/         Tests in C, plus a C++ compatibility test
mk/           Makefile modules
build/        Generated build tree (not in the repository)

Documentation
----------------------

The docs/ directory documents the whole repository in plain text, in
the style of this file.  Start with docs/README, which lists every
document and the reading order for a first-time reader.

License
----------------------

yupiter is multi licensed.  You may use, copy, modify and distribute
it under your choice of either license:

* GNU General Public License, version 2 or any later version (GPLv2+)
* GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or any later
  version (LGPLv2+)
* Apache License, Version 2.0 (Apache-2.0)

The LGPLv2+ option lets you link yupiter into your own program,
including a proprietary program, while keeping your program under
your own license; the obligations of the LGPL apply to the library
itself, not to the programs that use it.

The Apache-2.0 option is the right choice when your project must
link statically, for example because shared objects or DLLs are not
an option in your environment: it imposes no conditions on linking
at all, and grants an explicit patent license.

See the file LICENSE and the LICENSES/ directory for the full
texts and the documentation of the licensing scheme.

Contributing
---------------

Contributions, bug reports, and suggestions are welcome.

The public interface is stable, so a contribution that changes an
exported symbol, its signature, its C linkage, a public structure
layout or the documented behavior needs a very good reason and a new
major version.  Portability fixes, bug fixes and new tests are always
welcome.

Every change is built and tested on Linux for aarch64 and x86_64 by
GitHub Actions, with GCC and Clang, in Debug, RelWithDebInfo and
Release configurations, and with the static library, the shared
library and both.  A change that cannot be tested on a supported
target should say so explicitly.
